From 49facd55d4db2548041a34c3de2a3c9c8ab00390 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dev <dev@local>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 16:37:41 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 25/27] fix(debug): stop the stale service worker from blanking
 the app

The black-screen recorder caught its first real case, and the answer was in the
overlay it put on the screen:

    modulepreload failed to load: .../assets/BravaisCollection-Cys-1Rmu.js
    TypeError: Failed to fetch dynamically imported module: .../BravaisCollection-Cys-1Rmu.js
    React root has no children; the tree was unmounted

That chunk name does not exist in the installed APK (it ships
`BravaisCollection-6pBslNSm.js`), so the document that asked for it was older than
the APK: the service worker's precache still held the previous version's
index.html. On the web that is harmless - the old chunk files are still on the
server - but in a bundled shell they are GONE, so a stale document requests files
that cannot exist, the lazy import rejects, and with no error boundary React
unmounts the whole tree.

Two fixes, one for the cause and one for the class of failure:

- The Android shell no longer registers a service worker, and retires the one
  already on the device (unregister + drop `workbox-*` caches, leaving other
  origins' and features' Cache Storage alone). Offline in the shell is structural
  - the bundle is inside the APK - so the precache has nothing to add and a
  version skew to lose. Registration moved from vite-plugin-pwa's injected script
  (`injectRegister: false`) to src/index.tsx, because it now has to depend on the
  runtime. Web and desktop keep registering exactly as before.
- The root now has an error boundary, which this app never had. A failed lazy
  chunk means the page code is gone, so it reloads once (guarded by sessionStorage
  against a reload loop); anything else renders a readable message with a reload
  button instead of unmounting into a black screen.

Also corrected the recorder's label: a `modulepreload` link failure was reported
as "stylesheet failed to load", which pointed away from the actual cause.
